{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>He came from Sinai.</b> The Gaon [Saadia], of blessed memory, said that Mount Sinai, Seir, and Paran are near one another, and this verse concerns the standing at Mount Sinai. He said 'Sinai' because 'from Sinai' is like 'at Sinai,' and likewise 'from Seir.'" "<b>Myriads of holiness.</b> They are the angels, and the meaning concerns the Shekhinah that came down." "<b>A fiery law for them.</b> The Torah that was given in fire and lightning. And he [Saadia] said that the meaning of 'YHWH came from Sinai' is a way of prayer, as if saying: You who came at Sinai and bequeathed Torah — 'let Reuben live' (Deut. 33:6). And this is far-fetched, for it would have been appropriate to say 'may he live' and 'do not let him die.' And those lacking faith said that the meaning of 'from Seir' concerns the religion of Edom, and 'Paran' the religion of Ishmael — and these are deluded. Do they not see that he did not begin at the outset except to bless Israel alone, and so it is written: 'which Moses the man of Elohim blessed the children of Israel' (Deut. 33:1)? And he said: 'and He shone from Seir למו' (to them), whose meaning is 'to them,' like 'and there is no למו stumbling' (Deut. 7:25 [n: lit. 'no stumbling for them']). And the correct view in my eyes is that the passage is a general blessing for all Israel, and afterward he specified each tribe, and then gathered them all together at the place 'There is none like El, O Jeshurun' (Deut. 33:26). Now we find that Deborah said: 'YHWH, when You came forth from Seir, when You marched from the field of Edom' (Judg. 5:4). And behold, the field of Edom is not a mountain, as the Gaon said that the three mountains are adjacent or facing one another. And in the Book of Psalms [it is written]: 'O Elohim, when You went forth before Your people, when You marched through the wilderness, Selah' (Ps. 68:8), and after it is written: 'The earth shook' (Ps. 68:9). And the marching of the Shekhinah through the wilderness was not on the day of the giving of the Torah, for after the wilderness of Paran they came to the wilderness. And Habakkuk said: 'Eloah comes from Teman' (Hab. 1:3 [n: actually Hab. 3:3]), which is from the children of Edom — Teman, meaning like 'from the field of Edom.' Now behold, all of these speak of wars, and the witness: 'Let Elohim arise, let His enemies be scattered' (Num. 10:35). And when HaShem delivers His servants, Scripture likens the descent of aid from HaShem — and the earth melts, and the mountains dissolve, and the heavens quake. And the witness is in the songs of David: 'and the earth seethed and quaked' (2 Sam. 22:8). And the meaning of 'this Sinai' I have explained in its place. And so too the meaning is 'HaShem came,' that is, the Glory that entered among Israel, and the beginning of His entry was from Sinai, then shone from Seir. And the meaning is: all the years that Israel were in the wilderness, HaShem did not show might against the nations until Israel came to the field of Edom. And so it is written: 'in the heights of Seir, as far as El-Paran' (Gen. 14:6). And behold, Seir and Paran are near one another, and the meaning is explicit: from the place of Seir — when they came to Seir, then the Glory shone for Israel, and appeared from Mount Paran. And the meaning is doubled, for here He showed His radiance." "<b>And at His right hand, myriads of holiness.</b> In my opinion this is joined with 'from His right hand,' and the meaning is that fire came from myriads of holiness, from the right hand of HaShem, to surround Israel, like the meaning of 'horses of fire and chariots of fire surrounding Elisha' (2 Kings 6:17)." "And the meaning of <b>law</b> [דת] is that which stands forever."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {2 "Ibn Ezra cites Habakkuk 1:3 but the verse about Eloah coming from Teman is Habakkuk 3:3; translated the citation as he gives it without correction."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:26:33.290972838Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}